❯ Write a detailed 400 word story about a lighthouse keeper. Do not use tools.

⏺ The Keeper of Harrow Point

  Elias Venn had tended the light at Harrow Point for thirty-one years, and in that time he had learned that the sea was
  never silent. It hummed in calm weather and growled in storms, and on certain winter nights it seemed to whisper his
  name.

  Each evening he climbed the one hundred and twelve steps to the lantern room, his knees complaining on every landing.
  He polished the great Fresnel lens until it threw rainbows across the walls, trimmed the wick, and wound the clockwork
  that turned the beam. Then he sat with his tea and watched the dark come down over the water.

  He kept a logbook, as keepers always had. Most entries were brief. Wind northwest, fog by midnight. Light burning
  clear. But tucked between the weather notes, he wrote other things: the shape of a cloud, the cry of a gull, the day a
  seal pup had surfaced beside the rocks and stared up at him as if it knew him.

  On the night of the great storm, the logbook held only a single line. Waves higher than the gallery rail.

  The wind tore shingles from the keeper's cottage and hurled spray against the lantern glass. Elias gripped the rail
  and watched the beam cut through the chaos. Then, far out, he saw a smaller light flickering. A fishing boat, tossed
  like a cork, was struggling toward the rocks.








